WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:05.759
Long before Disney got involved with Marvel
and Star Wars flicks, the studio built its

00:00:05.759 --> 00:00:07.769
reputation on animated movies.

00:00:07.769 --> 00:00:14.129
Although their animated films tend to be blockbuster
releases and earn positive critical reviews,

00:00:14.129 --> 00:00:19.430
Disney's sequels are usually direct-to-video...and
decidedly lower quality.

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:23.250
Here are a few Disney sequels you might not
even know existed.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:25.560
"You're banning straight-to-video Disney films?"

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:26.560
"Absolutely.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:31.990
I mean, look at this, 'Aladdin 4: Jafar May
Need Glasses'."

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:33.960
Cinderella II: Dreams Come True

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:40.570
"And they lived happily ever after" certainly
sounds like a nice way to end an animated

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:44.730
fairy tale, but some people want to know what
comes after that.

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:51.149
That's why Disney decided to make a sequel
to Cinderella in 2002 - more than 50 years

00:00:51.149 --> 00:00:52.680
after the original film.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:58.050
It might've garnered an abysmal Rotten Tomatoes
score of 11 percent, but that didn't stop

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:03.690
the studio from making a third installment,
Cinderella III: A Twist in Time, which was

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:05.810
at least better than its predecessor.

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:12.510
It released in 2007, it miraculously earned
a Rotten Tomatoes score of 71 percent and

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:17.050
accumulated almost $92 million in home video
sales.

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:22.360
At least some Disney executives got their
happily ever after, even if our childhoods

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:23.760
didn't.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:25.710
Return to Never Land

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:30.470
Almost 50 years after the first Peter Pan
film released in theaters, Disney released

00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:33.900
a theatrical sequel, Return to Never Land.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:37.460
Just like the first film, the sequel is partially
based on J.M.

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:39.140
Barrie's Peter and Wendy.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:44.470
It follows the events of When Wendy Grew Up:
An Afterthought, an additional scene Barrie

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:46.720
added to his Peter Pan play.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:51.060
And that afterthought probably should have
stayed in Never Land.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:56.720
Return to Never Land received generally poor
reviews, but it still raked in almost $110

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:01.670
million worldwide on an estimated $20 million
production budget.

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:06.810
Disney opted not to pursue another sequel,
but the story did spawn the Jake and the Never

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:10.670
Land Pirates spinoff TV series on Disney Junior.

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:13.250
An Extremely Goofy Movie

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:18.920
This sequel to 1995's A Goofy Movie follows
Goofy and Max as they both head to college

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:24.820
to earn their undergraduate degrees…. which
already sounds like a pretty awful premise.

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:28.660
How did a guy like Goofy pass the basic admissions
process anyhow?

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:32.090
Are we supposed to assume it's on some kind
of musical scholarship?

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:36.950
Attending the same college as your Goofy father
isn't exactly the greatest idea in the world,

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:41.350
setting up all kinds of conflict and, well,
goofiness.

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:45.810
Since the films are technically a spinoff
of the TV series Goof Troop and continue the

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:51.610
story of Goofy and Max years later, An Extremely
Goofy Movie effectively acts as Goof Troop's

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:52.730
series finale.

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:58.550
Interestingly, the sequel is a rare case of
a direct-to-video Disney sequel rating slightly

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:04.930
higher than its predecessor, but by the year
2000, America's youth were just goofed out.

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:06.680
The Jungle Book 2

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:12.430
It's rare for a Disney sequel to open theatrically,
but most critics believe Disney would've been

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:18.200
better off just sending The Jungle Book 2
straight to DVD, or maybe the garbage.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:24.550
Produced by Disney Toon Studios, The Jungle
Book 2 released in 2003, 36 years after the

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:26.240
first Jungle Book movie.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:31.470
It starred John-Rhys Davies, Phil Collins,
and John Goodman, as well as young up-and-comers

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:33.800
Haley Joel Osment and Mae Whitman.

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:40.140
Despite being a worldwide box office success,
grossing $135 million on an estimated $20

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:45.360
million production budget, the film didn't
strike a chord with audiences the same way

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:46.980
the original film did.

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:53.640
Fortunately, it looks like Disney got back
on the critics' good side with 2016's live-action

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:54.770
remake.

00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:56.700
Kronk's New Groove

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:03.310
Disney released this direct-to-video sequel-slash-spinoff
to The Emperor's New Groove in 2005, five

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:05.350
years after the original film.

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:10.140
The sequel takes place after the events of
the first movie and follows Yzma's former

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:11.599
henchman, Kronk.

00:04:11.599 --> 00:04:15.860
That's not to say the Emperor isn't in the
movie at all — he actually narrates the

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:18.490
tale about Kronk's journey to win his father's
approval.

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:19.490
"I'm nice now!

00:04:19.490 --> 00:04:21.690
Didn't you see the first movie?

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:22.690
Yehehaaay!

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:24.750
A 50-foot me!"

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:29.350
Despite the talented voice cast — David
Spade, John Goodman, Eartha Kitt, and Patrick

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:34.050
Warburton — returning for the sequel, critics
heavily criticized the film for its lack of

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:37.389
originality and, well, groove.

00:04:37.389 --> 00:04:42.550
Whereas the original film received an Academy
Award nomination for Best Original Song, Kronk's

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:48.620
New Groove failed to produce even one decent
song... at least according to the critics.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:52.050
Beauty and the Beast: The Enchanted Christmas

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:57.500
Beauty and the Beast is one of the most beloved
Disney films of all time, with critics like

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:03.449
Roger Ebert hailing it as a magical tale in
the same league as Pinocchio and Snow White.

00:05:03.449 --> 00:05:08.350
Considering the first film was the first animated
movie ever to be nominated for Best Picture

00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:13.780
at the Academy Awards, it's disheartening
to see that its sequel was universally panned

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:14.820
by critics.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:19.550
Although technically a sequel, Beauty and
the Beast: The Enchanted Christmas takes place

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:24.610
within the events of the first Beauty and
the Beast film, not long after the Beast rescues

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:26.100
Belle from the wolves

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:31.389
Disney moved forward with yet another follow-up,
Beauty and the Beast: Belle's Magical World,

00:05:31.389 --> 00:05:36.380
one year after The Enchanted Christmas, set
shortly after the events of The Enchanted

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:37.380
Christmas.

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:42.190
As with the previous installment, Belle's
Magical World received poor reviews.

00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:46.860
Maybe two more movies about a monster with
rage issues and sassy appliances imprisoning

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:49.040
a lady was too much for kids.

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:50.040
Thanks for watching!

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:53.090
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:53.090 --> 00:05:55.430
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

